{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":40766872,"ns":0,"title":"Opioid excess theory","revisions":[{"revid":1366809278,"parentid":1360253061,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-07-30T10:46:22Z","size":11996,"sha1":"6e6f2b9a76eb9359b484a7835fa55c663f34919e","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Fringe autism causation theory}}\n{{More medical citations needed|date=July 2026}}\nThe '''opioid excess theory''' postulates that [[autism]] is the result of a [[metabolic disorder]] in which [[opioid peptide]]s produced through metabolism of [[gluten]] and [[casein]] pass through an abnormally [[intestinal permeability|permeable intestinal membrane]] and then proceed to exert an effect on neurotransmission through binding with [[opioid receptor]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Millward |first=Claire |last2=Ferriter |first2=Michael |last3=Calver |first3=Sarah J. |last4=Connell-Jones |first4=Graham G. |date=2019-04-02 |title=WITHDRAWN: Gluten- and casein-free diets for autistic spectrum disorder |journal=The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews |volume=4 |issue=4 |article-number=CD003498 |doi=10.1002/14651858.CD003498.pub4 |issn=1469-493X |pmc=6445047 |pmid=30938835}}</ref> Advocates of this hypothesis believe that autistic children are unusually sensitive to gluten, which results in [[enteritis|small bowel inflammation]] in these children, which in turn allows these opioid peptides to enter the brain.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.nhs.uk/news/2007/January08/Pages/Specialdietsandautism.aspx |title=Special diets and autism |access-date=2013-12-20 |archive-date=2013-12-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131221095932/http://www.nhs.uk/news/2007/January08/Pages/Specialdietsandautism.aspx }}</ref> However, mainstream reviews of existing studies have consistently determined there is insufficient evidence to validate the theory or prove that related [[Alternative therapies for developmental and learning disabilities|alternative therapies]] have a measurable impact on core autistic traits.\n\n==Early years==\nThis hypothesis was first proposed by [[Jaak Panksepp]] in a 1979 paper, in which he speculated that autism might be \"an emotional disturbance arising from an upset in the opiate systems in the brain\".<ref>{{Cite journal | last1 = Panksepp | first1 = J. | doi = 10.1016/0166-2236(79)90071-7 | title = A neurochemical theory of autism | journal = Trends in Neurosciences | volume = 2 | pages = 174–177 | year = 1979 | s2cid = 54373822 }}</ref> [[Kalle Reichelt]] then emerged as one of the leading advocates of this theory, publishing papers alleging that \"the patterns of peptides and associated proteins from urinary samples [from people with autism] differ considerably from each other and from normal controls.\" In addition, Reichelt's research has concluded that autistic individuals have increased levels of these peptides in their [[cerebrospinal fluid]].<ref>{{Cite journal \n| last1 = Reichelt | first1 = K. L. \n| last2 = Hole | first2 = K. \n| last3 = Hamberger | first3 = A. \n| last4 = Saelid | first4 = G. \n| last5 = Edminson | first5 = P. D. \n| last6 = Braestrup | first6 = C. B. \n| last7 = Lingjaerde | first7 = O. \n| last8 = Ledaal | first8 = P. \n| last9 = Orbeck | first9 = H. \n| display-authors = 3\n| title = Biologically active peptide-containing fractions in schizophrenia and childhood autism \n| journal = Advances in Biochemical Psychopharmacology \n| volume = 28 \n| pages = 627–643 \n| year = 1981 \n| pmid = 7010949\n}}</ref> Additionally, in a 1991 paper, Reichelt argued that [[gluten]] and [[casein]] may play a causative role in autism, as the incomplete digestion thereof may produce certain opioid peptides.<ref>[http://psycnet.apa.org/psycinfo/1993-26811-001 Probable etiology and possible treatment of childhood autism]</ref> Thus, those, such as [[Paul Shattock]], who advocate this theory also advocate the use of a [[gluten-free, casein-free diet]] as a treatment for autism.{{citation needed|date=April 2017}}\n\n==Wakefield study==\n{{main|Lancet MMR autism fraud}}\nIn 1998, a fraudulent paper by [[Andrew Wakefield]] was published in ''[[The Lancet]]'' presenting apparent evidence of a link between the [[MMR vaccine]], gastrointestinal disease and autism. In this paper, which has since been retracted, Wakefield et al. speculated that food-derived peptides \"may exert central-opioid effects, directly or through the formation of ligands with peptidase enzymes required for breakdown of endogenous central-nervous-system opioids, leading to disruption of normal neuroregulation and brain development by endogenous encephalins and endorphins\".<ref>{{Cite journal | last1 = Wakefield | first1 = A. J. | last2 = Murch | first2 = S. H. | last3 = Anthony | first3 = A. | last4 = Linnell | first4 = J. | last5 = Casson | first5 = D. M. | last6 = Malik | first6 = M. | last7 = Berelowitz | first7 = M. | last8 = Dhillon | first8 = A. P. | last9 = Thomson | first9 = M. A. | doi = 10.1016/S0140-6736(97)11096-0 | last10 = Harvey | first10 = P. | last11 = Valentine | first11 = A. | last12 = Davies | first12 = S. E. | last13 = Walker-Smith | first13 = J. A. | title = RETRACTED: Ileal-lymphoid-nodular hyperplasia, non-specific colitis, and pervasive developmental disorder in children | journal = The Lancet | volume = 351 | issue = 9103 | pages = 637–641 | year = 1998 | pmid =  9500320| s2cid = 439791 }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(10)60175-4|pmid=20137807|http://retractionwatch.com/the-retraction-watch-leaderboard/top-10-most-highly-cited-retracted-papers/ ''Retraction Watch''|http://retractionwatch.com/2015/02/03/frauds-long-tail-measles-outbreak-shows-important-look-downstream-retractions/ ''Retraction Watch''|intentional=yes}}</ref>\n\n==Later research==\nReichelt published a number of papers concluding that autistic children excrete higher levels of peptides in their urine,<ref>{{Cite journal | last1 = Knivsberg | first1 = A. M. | last2 = Reichelt | first2 = K. L. | last3 = Nødland | first3 = M. | last4 = Høien | first4 = T. | title = Autistic Syndromes and Diet: A follow-up study | doi = 10.1080/0031383950390304 | journal = Scandinavian Journal of Educational Research | volume = 39 | issue = 3 | pages = 223–236 | year = 1995 }}</ref> as well as that such peptides may cause autistic [[gaze aversion]]; specifically, by interfering with corticothalamocortical processing of visual stimuli.<ref>{{Cite journal \n| doi = 10.1159/000119167 \n| last1 = Lensing | first1 = P. \n| last2 = Schimke | first2 = H. \n| last3 = Klimesch | first3 = W. \n| last4 = Pap | first4 = V. \n| last5 = Szemes | first5 = G. \n| last6 = Klingler | first6 = D. \n| last7 = Panksepp | first7 = J. \n| title = Clinical case report: Opiate antagonist and event-related desynchronization in 2 autistic boys \n| journal = Neuropsychobiology \n| volume = 31 \n| issue = 1 \n| pages = 16–23 \n| year = 1995 \n| pmid = 7708177\n}}</ref> As a result of this theory, others, particularly Panksepp, have speculated that [[opioid antagonist]]s such as [[naloxone]] and [[naltrexone]] may be useful in the treatment of autism.<ref>{{Cite journal | last1 = Sahley | first1 = T. L. | last2 = Panksepp | first2 = J. | doi = 10.1007/BF01495056 | title = Brain opioids and autism: An updated analysis of possible linkages | journal = Journal of Autism and Developmental Disorders | volume = 17 | issue = 2 | pages = 201–216 | year = 1987 | pmid =  3038836| s2cid = 46566945 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal \n| doi = 10.1016/0165-1781(95)02601-R \n| last1 = Bouvard | first1 = M. P. \n| last2 = Leboyer | first2 = M. \n| last3 = Launay | first3 = J. M. \n| last4 = Recasens | first4 = C. \n| last5 = Plumet | first5 = M. H. \n| last6 = Waller-Perotte | first6 = D. \n| last7 = Tabuteau | first7 = F. \n| last8 = Bondoux | first8 = D. \n| last9 = Dugas | first9 = M. \n| last10 = Lensing | first10 = P. \n| last11 = Panksepp | first11 = J. \n| title = Low-dose naltrexone effects on plasma chemistries and clinical symptoms in autism: A double-blind, placebo-controlled study \n| journal = Psychiatry Research \n| volume = 58 \n| issue = 3 \n| pages = 191–201 \n| year = 1995 \n| pmid = 8570775\n| s2cid = 26253683 }}</ref> In addition, [[Christopher Gillberg]] of the [[University of Gothenburg]] has published some studies showing that animals treated with opiates exhibit less clinging, in line with the behavior of autistic children, who, his research has also shown, \"do not seem concerned when their parents are not near\" and \"exhibit less crying than infants without autism\",<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.macalester.edu/psychology/whathap/UBNRP/autismopioid/Opioids%20and%20Autism.htm |title=Social Dysfunction in Autism and Connection with Endogenous Opioids |website=www.macalester.edu |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030924053240/http://www.macalester.edu/psychology/whathap/UBNRP/autismopioid/Opioids%20and%20Autism.htm |archive-date=2003-09-24}} </ref> and has also linked an excess of endogenous opioids to stereotypic (i.e. repetitive) behavior.<ref>{{Cite journal \n| doi = 10.1111/j.1469-8749.1995.tb11998.x \n| last1 = Gillberg | first1 = C. \n| title = Endogenous opioids and opiate antagonists in autism: Brief review of empirical findings and implications for clinicians \n| journal = Developmental Medicine & Child Neurology \n| volume = 37 \n| issue = 3 \n| pages = 239–245 \n| year = 1995 \n| pmid = 7890130\n| s2cid = 28604551 }}</ref> However, more recently, two studies were published which failed to find a difference in levels of peptides in the urine of autistic children as opposed to those without autism.<ref>{{Cite journal | last1 = Hunter | first1 = L. C. | last2 = O'Hare | first2 = A. | last3 = Herron | first3 = W. J. | last4 = Fisher | first4 = L. A. | last5 = Jones | first5 = G. E. | title = Opioid peptides and dipeptidyl peptidase in autism | doi = 10.1111/j.1469-8749.2003.tb00915.x | journal = Developmental Medicine & Child Neurology | volume = 45 | issue = 2 | pages = 121–8 | year = 2007 | pmid =  12578238| doi-access =  | s2cid = 38949901 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal \n| last1 = Cass | first1 = H. \n| last2 = Gringras | first2 = P. \n| last3 = March | first3 = J. \n| last4 = McKendrick | first4 = I. \n| last5 = O'Hare | first5 = A. E. \n| last6 = Owen | first6 = L. \n| last7 = Pollin | first7 = C. \n| doi = 10.1136/adc.2006.114389 \n| title = Absence of urinary opioid peptides in children with autism \n| journal = Archives of Disease in Childhood \n| volume = 93 \n| issue = 9 \n| pages = 745–750 \n| year = 2008 \n| pmid = 18337276 \n| doi-access = free \n}}</ref> A 2009 review found that no evidence exists that urinary peptide levels are correlated with gut permeability.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Mulloy|first1=Austin|last2=Lang|first2=Russell|last3=O'Reilly|first3=Mark|last4=Sigafoos|first4=Jeff|last5=Lancioni|first5=Giulio|last6=Rispoli|first6=Mandy|title=Gluten-free and casein-free diets in the treatment of autism spectrum disorders: A systematic review|journal=Research in Autism Spectrum Disorders|date=July 2010|volume=4|issue=3|pages=328–339|doi=10.1016/j.rasd.2009.10.008}}</ref>\n\n==Possible implications for treatment==\nSeveral double blind studies experimented with low dose opioid antagonists, such as [[naltrexone]], for treatment of autism. A 2014 systematic review showed statistically significant improvement in symptoms of irritability and hyperactivity in 77% of children treated with naltrexone. Core autism symptoms were unaffected. Side effects were mild and the drug was generally well tolerated. The number of children undergoing such therapy in the 10 analysed studies was 128.<ref>{{Cite journal\n | pmid = 24589346\n| year = 2014\n| last1 = Roy\n| first1 = A\n| title = Are opioid antagonists effective in attenuating the core symptoms of autism spectrum conditions in children: A systematic review\n| journal = Journal of Intellectual Disability Research\n| pages = 293–306\n| last2 = Roy\n| first2 = M\n| last3 = Deb\n| first3 = S\n| last4 = Unwin\n| first4 = G\n| last5 = Roy\n| first5 = A\n| doi = 10.1111/jir.12122\n | volume=59\n| issue = 4\n| s2cid = 11896817\n}}</ref> \n\n==References==\n{{Reflist|30em}}\n\n{{Opioidergics}}\n[[Category:Autism pseudoscience]]\n[[Category:Alternative therapies for developmental and learning disabilities]]\n[[Category:Causes of autism]]\n[[Category:Opioids]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-26T19:54:43Z","lastrevid":1366809278,"length":11996}]}}